61	\subsection{Decoupling}\label{ssec:decoupling}
62	
63	As aggregate context for the per-example findings, Table~\ref{tab:convergence} reports cross-architecture $\sigma$ across benchmarks at 1.3B. Only LAMBADA shows monotonic convergence ($\rho = -1.0$, Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$, adj-$p = .170$ under all-10-benchmark correction, Appendix~A; excluding GDN: $\rho = -0.7$; observed at 1.3B, absent at 340M; best interpreted as exploratory given $n = 5$; Appendix~\ref{app:convergence_detail}, Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}). No other benchmark shows significant convergence. This raises the question: does LAMBADA's apparent aggregate convergence reflect genuine per-example agreement?
64	
65	It does not. Aggregate convergence and per-example divergence operate on different timescales as attention ratio increases (Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling}).
66	
67	Cross-architecture $\sigma$ decreases gradually across the five attention ratios: $0.024 \to 0.016 \to 0.014 \to 0.006 \to 0.005$. In contrast, pairwise Jaccard similarity between architectures' per-example effects saturates at the first attention level (4\%): approximately 0.095 for helped, with no further increase at higher ratios. Net asymmetry between architectures' per-example profiles increases from 0.013 at 4\% attention to 0.040 at 25\%. If convergence arose from attention uniformly rescuing the same difficult examples across architectures, Jaccard would increase in parallel with decreasing $\sigma$. The observed saturation of Jaccard at the first attention level indicates that each architecture's per-example response to attention is largely idiosyncratic. The observed $\bar{J} \approx 0.10$ is far below the theoretical maximum $J_{\max} \approx 0.78$--$0.92$ given helped-set sizes, ruling out ceiling constraint
